Rationale:
A course is a food or group of foods served or intended to be eaten at one time;

A menu is the list of offerings. 

A meal is an occasion when food is eaten.

The specific population group to be monitored is correct because defining the target population is a foundational design decision that shapes every other aspect of a surveillance system, including what indicators to collect and how. 

• The exact number of individuals to include is more of a sampling/statistical detail; 
• The optimal time of day for data collection and ideal food sample storage temperature are narrow, operational/logistical details.

Simple diffusion - Water and small lipids cross into intestinal cells freely;
Facilitated diffusion - water soluble vitamins need a specific carrier to transport them from one side of the cell membrane to the other;
Active transport - glucose and amino acids move against a concentration gradient, which requires energy;
Pinocytosis, a form of endocytosis, in which material enters a cell through its membrane and is incorporated in vesicles for digestion.

Polymeric formulas contain intact, whole macronutrients, including intact proteins, carbohydrates, and fats. They are designed for patients with a functioning gastrointestinal tract who can digest and absorb these nutrients adequately. They are the most commonly used type of standard enteral formula.

Intact CPF = Polymeric;
Broken down = Hydrolyzed;
One nutrient at a time = Modular;
Specific disease = Specialty

Dried beans are considered non-perishable foods because their low moisture content allows them to be stored for long periods at room temperature without rapid spoilage, provided they are kept in proper dry-storage conditions.

• Potatoes: semi-perishable; 
• Fresh milk and fresh meat: perishable
